{"beat": 6, "name": "WHAT IT'S WORTH", "text": "What's the difference worth? Every decision that used the wrong margin. Reorders first. A product with a high gross margin and a thin contribution gets restocked deep, and the cash sits on a shelf. The newsvendor on this catalog finds $3,874 a month of fees on stock ordered past its economic quantity. Ads next. The break-even return on ad spend is set by contribution, never by gross. On this catalog it's 2.61. The worst campaign, Catalog, Auto, returns 0.97 on its last dollar, which is below break-even at contribution and looks perfectly healthy if you judge it at gross. Pricing. A price rise on a product with a fee tier in the way can lift gross margin and cut contribution in the same move, and only the contribution waterfall shows it. And concentration. There are 24 products on the shelf, but by where the money comes from the catalog behaves like 19.3, so the risk of the business sits in fewer products than the shelf suggests. In short: the margin you quote and the margin you keep are 38.4% of revenue apart on this catalog, and every reorder, bid and price set on the wrong one was set on a number that's wrong by that much.", "provider": "library", "words": [{"word": "What's", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.267}, {"word": "the", "start": 0.313, "end": 0.383}, {"word": "difference", "start": 0.43, "end": 0.801}, {"word": "worth?", "start": 0.836, "end": 1.416}, {"word": "Every", "start": 2.229, "end": 2.543}, {"word": "decision", "start": 2.601, "end": 3.019}, {"word": "that", "start": 3.065, "end": 3.17}, {"word": "used", "start": 3.239, "end": 3.553}, {"word": "the", "start": 3.587, "end": 3.68}, {"word": "wrong", "start": 3.727, "end": 4.029}, {"word": "margin.", "start": 4.11, "end": 4.714}, {"word": "Reorders", "start": 5.097, "end": 5.619}, {"word": "first.", "start": 5.689, "end": 6.246}, {"word": "A", "start": 6.571, "end": 6.653}, {"word": "product", "start": 6.734, "end": 7.082}, {"word": "with", "start": 7.105, "end